29	declare_source_rule! {
30	    /// Sorts imports and exports in your JavaScript and TypeScript files.
31	    ///
32	    /// By default, imports and exports are sorted by "distance" from the current file:
33	    ///
34	    /// 1. URLs such as `https://example.org`.
35	    /// 2. Packages with a protocol such as `node:path`, `bun:test`, `jsr:@my?lib`, or `npm:lib`.
36	    /// 3. Packages such as `mylib` or `@my/lib`.
37	    /// 4. Aliases: sources starting with `@/`, `#`, `~`, `$`, or `%`.
38	    ///    They usually are [Node.js subpath imports](https://nodejs.org/api/packages.html#subpath-imports) or [TypeScript path aliases](https://www.typescriptlang.org/tsconfig/#paths).
39	    /// 5. Absolute and relative paths.
40	    ///
41	    /// Imports and exports with the same distance are sorted using a
42	    /// [natural sort order](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_sort_order)
43	    /// such that `A < a < a9 < a10 < B < b`
44	    ///
45	    /// The action also merges imports and exports from the same source,
46	    /// sorts named specifiers and attributes using a natural sort order.
47	    ///
48	    /// For example, the following code...
49	    ///
50	    /// ```js,ignore
51	    /// import sibling from "./file.js";
52	    /// import internal from "#alias";
53	    /// import fs from "fs";
54	    /// import { test } from "node:test";
55	    /// import path from "node:path";
56	    /// import { mock } from "node:test";
57	    /// import parent from "../parent.js";
58	    /// import scopedLibUsingJsr from "jsr:@scoped/lib";
59	    /// import data from "https://example.org";
60	    /// import { B, A, b, a10, a9 } from "lib";
61	    /// import scopedLib from "@scoped/lib";
62	    /// export { Y } from "dep";
63	    /// export * from "./inner.js";
64	    /// export { X } from "dep";
65	    /// ```
66	    ///
67	    /// ...is sorted as follows:
68	    ///
69	    /// ```js,ignore
70	    /// import data from "https://example.org";
71	    /// import scopedLibUsingJsr from "jsr:@scoped/lib";
72	    /// import path from "node:path";
73	    /// import { mock, test } from "node:test";
74	    /// import scopedLib from "@scoped/lib";
75	    /// import fs from "fs";
76	    /// import { A, a9, a10, B, b } from "lib";
77	    /// import internal from "#alias";
78	    /// import parent from "../parent.js";
79	    /// import sibling from "./file.js";
80	    ///
81	    /// export { X, Y } from "dep";
82	    /// export * from "./inner.js";
83	    /// ```
84	    ///
85	    /// ## Options
86	    ///
87	    /// The action provides several options to customize how imports and exports are ordered:
88	    ///
89	    /// - `groups` allows to group imports and exports before sorting them;
90	    ///   It allows expressing custom order between imports or exports.
91	    /// - `sortBareImports` allows sorting bare imports (also called side-effect imports);
92	    ///   By default the action doesn't sort them.
93	    /// - `identifierOrder` allows changing how named specifiers and attributes are sorted
94	    ///
95	    /// ### `groups`
96	    ///
97	    /// You can customize how imports and exports are grouped using the `groups` option.
98	    /// The option accepts an array of group matchers,
99	    /// which in their simplest form are glob patterns or predefined group matchers.
100	    /// Imports and exports that don't match any group are automatically moved after all the groups.
101	    ///
102	    /// Groups are always matched in order, so earlier matchers take priority.
103	    /// To exclude some imports of a group, you can use an array of group matchers
104	    /// with negated matchers, prefixed with `!`.
105	    /// In the following example, we use the negated glob matcher `!@myown/**`,
106	    /// to exclude `@myown/package` from the `:PACKAGE:` group.
107	    ///
108	    /// With this configuration...
109	    ///
110	    /// ```json,options
111	    /// {
112	    ///    "options": {
113	    ///         "groups": [
114	    ///             [":BUN:", ":NODE:"],
115	    ///             ":BLANK_LINE:",
116	    ///             [":PACKAGE:", "!@myown/**"],
117	    ///             ":BLANK_LINE:",
118	    ///             "@myown/**",
119	    ///             ":BLANK_LINE:",
120	    ///             [":ALIAS:", ":PATH:"]
121	    ///         ]
122	    ///     }
123	    /// }
124	    /// ```
125	    ///
126	    /// ...the following code...
127	    ///
128	    /// ```js,ignore
129	    /// import aliased from "@/components/Button";
130	    /// import lib from "lib";
131	    /// import path from "node:path";
132	    /// import sibling from "./file.js";
133	    /// import myown from "@myown/package";
134	    /// import scopedLib from "@scoped/lib";
135	    /// import fs from "fs";
136	    /// ```
137	    ///
138	    /// ...is sorted as:
139	    ///
140	    /// ```js,ignore
141	    /// import fs from "fs";
142	    /// import path from "node:path";
143	    ///
144	    /// import scopedLib from "@scoped/lib";
145	    /// import lib from "lib";
146	    ///
147	    /// import myown from "@myown/package";
148	    ///
149	    /// import aliased from "@/components/Button";
150	    /// import sibling from "./file.js";
151	    /// ```
152	    ///
153	    /// Each entry in the `groups` array is a group matcher that can be:
154	    ///
155	    /// - A predefined group like `:NODE:`, `:BUN:`, or `:PACKAGE:`
156	    /// - A glob pattern like `@my/lib/**`;
157	    ///   the action supports a [limited set of globs](#supported-glob-patterns).
158	    /// - Type-only imports like `{ "type": true }`
159	    /// - A combination of the above, e.g. `[":BUN:", ":NODE:"]`
160	    /// - `:BLANK_LINE:` to insert a blank line between groups
161	    ///
162	    /// #### Predefined groups
163	    ///
164	    /// - `:URL:`: sources starting with `https://` or `http://`
165	    /// - `:NODE:`: Node.js built-in modules (`node:path`, `fs`, `path`, etc.)
166	    /// - `:BUN:`: Bun built-in modules (`bun:test`, `bun`, etc.)
167	    /// - `:PACKAGE_WITH_PROTOCOL:`: packages with a protocol (`jsr:@my/lib`, `npm:lib`)
168	    /// - `:PACKAGE:`: bare and scoped packages (`lib`, `@scoped/lib`)
169	    /// - `:ALIAS:`: path aliases starting with `#`, `@/`, `~`, `$`, or `%`
170	    /// - `:PATH:`: absolute and relative paths
171	    /// - `:STYLE:`: paths ending with the following style extensions:
172	    ///   `.css`, `.less`, `.pcss`, `.sass`, `.scss`, `.sss` and `.styl`
173	    ///
174	    /// #### Type-only matcher
175	    ///
176	    /// Use a type-only matcher to separate `import type` from regular imports:
177	    /// Setting `"type": true` matches only `import type` and `export type` statements.
178	    /// Setting `"type": false` matches only non-type imports and exports.
179	  
